{"archetypes":[{"name":"no-op-claim-auditor","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan silently drops two items as evidence-backed no-ops; if those claims are wrong a needed fix is never made.","prompt_body":"The plan declares two items Already-resolved with no edits: (1) that approval-gates.md:209 no longer references LARCH_DESIGN_CONVERGENCE_THRESHOLD, and (2) that cleanup.md already documents the cache/tmp find predicate asymmetry. Read the current state of both files at the cited locations and verify each no-op claim is actually true. If a claim is false, report the exact line content that contradicts it and flag the dropped fix as a plan gap. Also check whether the plan's rationale for each no-op is internally consistent with the diff context it cites (e.g., which PR number removed what). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"convergence-sweep","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan enumerates specific files to purge --convergence-threshold; a missed occurrence leaves a now-rejected flag in a caller and causes silent exit-2 failures.","prompt_body":"The plan removes --convergence-threshold from a fixed list of files: run-step3-review.sh, run-step3-review.md, SKILL.md, flags.md, docs/configuration-and-permissions.md, test-run-step3-review.sh, test-step3-review-cap.sh, and test-design-multi-round-integration.sh. Grep the entire repo (skills/, scripts/, docs/, .claude/) for the literal strings convergence-threshold and LARCH_DESIGN_CONVERGENCE_THRESHOLD and verify that every occurrence found is covered by the plan's file list. Flag any file that contains a reference but is absent from the plan's update list, and check whether approval-gates.md, plan-review-loop.sh, and test-plan-review-loop.sh are correctly excluded or deliberately left unchanged. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-tempfile-contract","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The cleanup.sh change introduces a mktemp+if-find wrap under set -e; subtle ordering or rm-f omissions can make a supposed fail-safe silently abort cleanup and suppress KV output.","prompt_body":"The plan wraps each find enumeration pass in a guarded mktemp allocation followed by an if-find branch, with rm -f on every exit path. Review the described code shape against Bash 3.2 constraints from BASH_AUTHORING.md: verify that the mktemp guard (if _list=$(mktemp …)) is compatible with set -e and that rm -f $_cache_list appears on the allocation-failure path, the find-failure path, and the success path. Verify the NUL-safe while-read loop retains || true so loop-body failures (e.g., rm of a session dir) do not exit the script. Check whether the test stub discriminant (-mindepth 1 as the key distinguishing enumeration find from the nested-scan find using -maxdepth 5) is actually unambiguous given the symlink-reaper find at -maxdepth 1 without -mindepth. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
